03. Data Ownership, Timeline, and Evidence
OriginalAsset
•	Store the original file identity, full checksum, actual sample rate, channel layout, sample count, and duration.
•	Preserve original file bytes.
•	Record decoding, resampling, or channel conversion as separately identified derivatives with their own metadata and checksums.
•	Report unsupported formats or channel layouts explicitly.
•	Never silently downmix, truncate, pad, or relabel a derivative as original.
ReferenceShape
•	Store serializable performance evidence sufficient for source-free synthesis:
o	note and gesture onsets and offsets
o	pitch and partial trajectories, including bends, slides, and vibrato
o	amplitude envelopes, dynamics, accents, and repeated excitation
o	spectral envelopes, harmonic distributions, and supported formant traits
o	supported noise-texture parameters
o	attack, decay, sustain, damping, and release behavior
o	overlapping ringing and energy carried between gestures
o	evidence classifications, competing hypotheses, and unresolved spans.
ReferenceShape ownership
•	Keep measured evidence separate from editable rendering instructions.
•	Changing source voicing or downstream settings MUST preserve the reference shape hash.
•	Explicit reanalysis creates a new reference-shape revision.
Evidence classifications
•	Preserve supported evidence, uncertain evidence, out-of-target evidence, and silence as distinct states.
•	Preserve evidence strength separately so weak coherent evidence remains distinguishable from strongly supported evidence.
•	Retain competing pitch hypotheses and documented confidence information.
Evidence interpretation
•	A click alone does not establish a pick attack.
•	Unexplained energy does not establish noise or another instrument.
•	Pitch fluctuation does not establish intentional expression or recording damage without supporting evidence.
•	Published instrument ranges cannot invalidate supported out-of-range cues.
Analysis
•	Use time-frequency analysis at appropriate temporal and spectral resolutions.
•	Follow coherent ridges, harmonic relationships, diagonal pitch movement, quiet attacks, amplitude evolution, and ringing across gesture boundaries.
•	Separate suspected recording artifacts from supported performance details.
•	Preserve ambiguity around bleed, shimmer, watery texture, crackle, separation smear, and wow/flutter.
CuePacket
•	Save editable rendering instructions, reference-shape identity, uncertainty, source-model configuration, deterministic seeds, and synthesis parameters.
•	Exclude original PCM, original phase, copied residuals, and source-file access capabilities from the synthesis-facing packet.
GeneratedAudio
•	Use immutable audio buffers with sample rate, channel layout, frame count, logical timeline origin, content hash, render revision, and provenance.
•	Publish a new buffer when audio changes.
•	Never mutate an original asset or an active playback buffer in place.
Timeline
•	Represent committed audio boundaries using integer sample frames.
•	Preserve the imported performance timeline throughout the pipeline.
•	Record physical propagation delay separately from algorithmic latency.
•	Record any output tail explicitly.
•	At an unchanged sample rate, foundational dry output MUST cover the complete reference frame count rather than an implicitly selected excerpt.
