04. Stage 1 — Dry Regeneration
Analysis inputs
•	OriginalAsset plus explicitly selected analysis settings.
Synthesis inputs
•	ReferenceShape, derived cue instructions, source-character settings, implemented source-model parameters, render format, and deterministic seed.
Output
•	A complete, newly synthesized, completely dry take.
•	A saved cue packet.
•	A render record identifying effective settings and unresolved evidence.
•	An immutable cached dry buffer.
Dry definition
•	Dry synthesis contains intrinsic source behavior such as string ringing, body resonance, supported breath texture, and supported attack character.
•	Dry synthesis contains no virtual microphone coloration, propagation delay, room reflections, material absorption, spatial processing, or plugin effects.
Synthesis structure
•	Use a DDSP-style control structure with source-appropriate harmonic, modal, and freshly generated noise components.
•	A procedural implementation is sufficient.
•	Training on external music catalogs is not required.
Harmonic and modal synthesis
•	Drive generated oscillator or modal frequencies from supported trajectories.
•	Generate phase internally.
•	Preserve continuous generated phase through supported slides and bends.
•	Represent repeated excitation without unnecessarily resetting ringing state.
•	Preserve supported overlap rather than forcing every source into one monophonic fundamental track.
Noise synthesis
•	Generate supported breath, bow, friction, or related texture from compact parameters and reproducible noise generation.
•	Never copy the source residual.
•	Never fill unresolved structure with generic noise.
Unresolved synthesis
•	Render supported components without asserting unsupported fundamental, articulation, or instrument identities.
•	Leave unresolved components explicitly identified in the render record.
•	Do not create new attacks or notes solely to bridge uncertain spans.
Source-character guidance
•	Use the selected source model to parameterize harmonic weighting, formants, body response, texture, and characteristic decay.
•	Preserve evidence-supported timing, pitch, phrasing, dynamics, and overlap.
•	Follow supported unusual behavior even when a generic model predicts a different response.
Character controls
•	Support keeping the observed character or applying a selected Natural, Analog, or Digital character target.
•	Retain supported source-character variants from the reference catalog.
•	Apply bounded, visible guidance strengths.
•	Clearing guidance returns to shape-driven synthesis.
•	Character selection MUST NOT silently enable microphone, room, or plugins.
•	Analog character MUST NOT be represented by automatically adding hum.
Rough character estimation
•	Compare measurable noise-floor behavior, spectral balance, distortion, apparent room energy, and related traits with loose reference profiles.
•	Present Natural, Analog, Digital, or Unclear with uncertainty information.
•	Treat source-family, microphone-style, and room-style matches as suggestions.
•	Require an explicit accepted target before a suggestion changes processing.
•	Never present profile similarity as identification of the original setup.
Global straightening
•	Keep light straightening disabled until explicitly requested.
•	Apply requested mild pitch-wander or consistency correction only to derived dry-synthesis instructions.
•	Preserve supported bends, slides, vibrato, unusual articulation, and timing.
•	Do not require transcription, MIDI conversion, quantization, or ideal notation.
Cache identity
•	Include reference-shape revision, effective dry settings, source-model data, renderer version, sample format, and deterministic seed.
Bypass
•	Return an existing valid generated dry cache for the active reference and effective dry configuration.
•	With no valid generated dry cache, report DryUnavailable and block dependent rendering or export.
•	While regeneration is bypassed, changed dry controls remain visibly pending.
•	Cached-output metadata MUST continue to identify the settings that actually produced the audible buffer.
•	Reenabling regeneration applies pending settings and creates a new revision.
05. Stage 2 — Surgical Region Editing
Inputs
•	The current Stage 1 dry buffer.
•	ReferenceShape and corresponding derived synthesis instructions.
•	Region definitions, explicit fix requests, and accepted edit history.
Output
•	The current edited dry take.
•	Versioned accepted edit records and localized candidate metadata.
•	The unchanged Stage 1 buffer remains available.
Region tools
•	Provide rectangular marquee selection on dry spectral views.
•	Provide draggable in/out ranges on dry waveform and spectral views.
•	Support moving rectangles and resizing every rectangle edge and corner.
•	Support multiple independent regions.
•	Store time-range or time-frequency bounds relative to the dry timeline.
•	Optional lasso selection MUST follow the same bounded-edit contract.
Selection ownership
•	Enable correction tools only on the dry take.
•	Display the original beside the dry take for reference.
•	Creating, moving, resizing, annotating, or deleting a selection changes selection metadata only.
•	Selection manipulation MUST NOT commit audio.
Fix requests
•	Allow a short note and a light straighten, smooth, reduce-noise, or stabilize request for the active region.
•	Resolve a free description only to supported bounded operations.
•	Preserve an unsupported description as a visible unresolved request.
•	Never execute undocumented processing from an ambiguous note.
Candidate generation
•	Reprocess only selected derived shape components through the dry synthesizer.
•	Read surrounding context when necessary for continuity.
•	Keep surrounding audio and unselected shape components unchanged.
•	Bind each candidate to the exact dry input hash, region revision, committed bounds, fix request, synthesis configuration, and uncertainty state.
Frequency scope
•	Limit directly modified components or coefficients to the selected frequency mask.
•	Never convert a frequency-limited request into an undisclosed full-band edit.
•	Reject an operation unchanged when the requested operation cannot honor the selected scope.
•	Do not claim perfect spectral isolation from a finite temporal edit.
Temporal scope
•	Commit changes only inside [start_frame, end_frame_exclusive).
•	Place short boundary crossfades entirely inside the selected time interval.
•	Bound each crossfade by the available region length.
•	Blend newly synthesized regional audio with generated dry audio only.
•	Preserve samples outside the committed time interval bit-for-bit.
•	Never normalize or reprocess the complete dry take to accommodate a patch.
Acceptance
•	Require explicit Apply or equivalent acceptance before replacing committed dry audio.
•	Reject stale candidates.
•	Store accepted edit bounds independently from mutable selection geometry.
•	Moving or deleting a selection after acceptance MUST NOT relocate or undo previously accepted audio.
Overlapping edits
•	Apply accepted edits against the current committed dry revision.
•	Record acceptance order and parent/output revisions.
•	Never overwrite a newer accepted edit with a candidate from an older parent.
Undo/redo
•	Maintain independent history for selection changes and accepted resynthesis.
•	Restore validated dry revisions when undoing or redoing accepted audio edits.
•	Preserve other region definitions when one region changes.
Upstream regeneration
•	Invalidate candidate audio and accepted patch buffers when Stage 1 changes.
•	Preserve region definitions and fix requests.
•	Require reprocessing against the new dry revision before patches become current.
•	Never splice patch audio from a different dry revision into the new take.
Region preview
•	Support region solo, optional looping, and same-bounds before/candidate A/B.
•	Debounce candidate regeneration while an active requested region is moved or resized.
•	Replace only the relevant preview buffer.
•	Preserve full-take transport position and unrelated playback.
•	Use the dry preview path by default.
•	Apply microphone or room processing to region audition only when explicitly enabled for audition.
•	Keep every preview result separate from committed dry audio.
Downstream scheduling
•	Selection changes and unaccepted candidates leave committed downstream results valid.
•	Accepted dry changes mark downstream results stale.
•	Defer downstream rerendering during dry editing until the user leaves dry editing or explicitly refreshes later stages.
•	Prevent stale downstream output from being exported as current.
Bypass
•	Return Stage 1 audio unchanged.
•	Preserve region definitions, requests, and accepted edit history.
•	Reenabling surgical processing requires edits valid for the current base.
•	An empty edit set behaves as identity processing.
