06. Stage 3 — Optional Microphone
Default
•	Bypassed.
Inputs
•	The current Stage 2 dry output.
•	Explicit source and receiver geometry.
•	Selected microphone profile, pattern, aim, and implemented response.
•	Explicit source-radiation parameters when an implemented model exists.
Output
•	A direct microphone capture buffer.
•	An explicit capture context for Stage 4.
Enabled behavior
•	Apply the selected implemented microphone response.
•	Apply direct-path distance, propagation delay, and directional pickup.
•	Preserve the underlying dry performance and reference shape.
•	Support existing independent microphone slots for the same single source.
•	Preserve explicit channel or slot routing without an undocumented mic sum.
Capture context
•	Carry the exact immutable generated dry excitation consumed by this stage.
•	Carry the direct processed buffer.
•	Carry source/receiver poses and microphone-transfer snapshots.
•	Carry direct-path delay, gain, filtering, timeline, and routing information.
•	Record which direct-path operations have already been applied.
Context ownership
•	Stage 4 MUST receive the capture context through the Stage 3 output.
•	Stage 4 MUST NOT obtain another dry buffer through global state.
•	The capture context MUST contain no original PCM or source-file capability.
Bypass
•	Return the Stage 2 audible buffer unchanged.
•	Expose a neutral pickup in the capture context.
•	Retain receiver positions for an independently enabled room stage.
•	Disable microphone coloration, microphone directional weighting, and microphone-specific response behavior.
•	Stored microphone model or pattern changes cannot affect room-only audio.
Parameter ownership
•	Microphone-specific controls belong to this stage.
•	Source and receiver positions belong to shared physical scene state.
•	A shared placement change may affect an enabled room stage even when the microphone stage is bypassed.
07. Stage 4 — Optional Space / Room
Default
•	Bypassed, with a configured 10 × 10 × 10 ft padded room.
Inputs
•	The complete Stage 3 output and capture context.
•	Room topology, physical dimensions, independent surface materials, source/receiver geometry, and acoustic model parameters.
Output
•	A spatially processed capture buffer with explicit propagation and tail metadata.
Enabled behavior
•	Compute direct and reflected paths from actual geometry.
•	Combine room dimensions, materials, source placement, receiver placement, source radiation, and effective pickup weighting.
•	Produce spatial depth in the rendered audio before export.
Direct path
•	With microphone processing enabled, retain the Stage 3 direct result once.
•	With microphone processing bypassed, compute the room's direct propagation using the generated excitation and neutral receiver.
Reflected paths
•	Build each reflected contribution from the generated excitation carried in the Stage 3 context.
•	Apply full path delay, distance attenuation, surface reflection factors, source-radiation weighting, and effective microphone pickup once per path.
•	Avoid applying microphone response, direct delay, or attenuation twice.
•	Never attempt to recover excitation by dividing through microphone nulls.
Room topology
•	Closed mode uses the configured floor, ceiling, and four walls.
•	Open mode uses the ground only.
•	Open placement guides MUST NOT generate invisible walls or ceiling.
Performance preservation
•	Spatial processing MUST NOT change cue pitches, note decisions, timing instructions, articulation, or evidence classifications.
•	Physical propagation and reflections remain capture effects.
Bypass
•	Return the Stage 3 audible buffer unchanged, including timeline and length.
•	Preserve room configuration.
•	Ignore room reflection state and tails while bypassed.
•	Permit room processing independently of microphone processing.
08. Stage 5 — Optional Plugin Slot
Default
•	Empty and bypassed.
Inputs
•	The current Stage 4 output.
•	An explicitly selected compatible processor and saved processor settings.
Output
•	The processed final buffer with processor identity, settings, latency, tail behavior, and provenance.
Processing
•	Permit compatible EQ, dynamics, noise-control, or finishing processing through the existing optional slot.
•	Keep SpectraCraft's host and integration code in Rust.
•	Evaluate compatibility, licensing, and reproducibility before integration.
•	Do not require a particular external service, library, or processor.
Isolation
•	Plugins receive only predecessor audio and declared processing context.
•	Plugins MUST NOT access original audio, reenter extraction, rewrite shape evidence, receive surgical-edit commands, or modify upstream dry buffers.
State
•	Initialize processor state explicitly for offline renders.
•	Reset processing history on bypass transitions or input-revision changes.
•	Account for declared algorithmic latency without removing intentional acoustic propagation delay.
Bypass
•	Return Stage 4 audio unchanged.
•	An empty slot behaves identically to bypass.
09. Render Dependencies and Cache Invalidation
Dependency graph
•	Reference analysis → Stage 1 → Stage 2 → Stage 3 → Stage 4 → Stage 5.
Dry-setting change
•	Invalidate Stage 1 and every dependent result.
Accepted surgical change or surgical bypass change
•	Preserve Stage 1.
•	Invalidate Stage 2 and dependent results.
Microphone-setting change
•	Preserve reference evidence and both dry stages.
•	Invalidate applicable Stage 3 and dependent results.
Room/material change
•	Preserve dry and microphone results when their direct-path inputs remain unchanged.
•	Invalidate Stage 4 and Stage 5.
Shared physical placement change
•	Invalidate every enabled capture stage that consumes the changed geometry.
•	Preserve analysis and dry synthesis.
Plugin-setting change
•	Invalidate Stage 5 only.
Bypassed stage-specific setting change
•	Preserve active audio.
•	Save the changed setting for explicit reenablement.
Display or selection-only change
•	Invalidate no committed audio result.
Cache records
•	Include upstream content identity, effective stage settings, renderer/model version, format, and required deterministic state.
•	Distinguish saved controls from effective controls when changes are pending.
•	Include auxiliary capture-context identity in room cache keys.
Render jobs
•	Capture immutable input snapshots and monotonically ordered request IDs.
•	Discard obsolete completions.
•	Publish results only after finite-sample, format, and revision validation.
•	Never label a previous revision as the latest result.
Stage failures
•	Report the failed stage and retain explicit error state.
•	Never substitute original audio, silently bypass an enabled stage, or relabel stale output as successful processing.
10. Source, Microphone, Material, and Profile Data
Catalog storage
•	Load structured local data from the capture-reference package.
•	Preserve every recovered entry without silent filtering or truncation.
•	Compare loaded counts against the actual package manifest.
•	Treat the documented 138 sources, approximately 972 microphones, and 32 materials as inventory checks, never hard-coded capacity limits.
•	Support adding or correcting compatible data entries without engine changes.
•	Require no runtime connection to catalog websites.
Provenance
•	Preserve source documents, reference URLs, measurement or modeling status, uncertainty, and missing fields.
•	Keep published facts separate from effective renderer parameters.
•	Never invent missing measurements or precise resonance values.
•	Label generic approximations explicitly.
•	Disclose when different catalog entries share an implemented response.
Source entry fields
•	Family and source name.
•	Published fundamental and practical playing ranges.
•	Broad body, presence, attack, and high-frequency regions.
•	Known resonance, formant, harmonic, and decay characteristics.
•	Synthesis-model hint.
•	Available provenance and missing-data flags.
Microphone entry fields
•	Brand, model, and transducer type.
•	Primary and supported pickup patterns.
•	Published response points or response-document reference.
•	Available frequency-dependent polar data or document reference.
•	Sensitivity, self-noise, maximum SPL, frequency range, and power requirements.
•	Manufacturer/source documents, uncertainty, and missing fields.
•	The implemented DSP response and pattern mapping, recorded separately.
Microphone model rules
•	Catalog selection MUST load associated data and effective renderer mapping.
•	Published response data affect DSP only through an explicitly implemented response model.
•	A catalog name or polar image cannot establish measured hardware emulation.
•	Unsupported response data MUST remain reference information.
Material entry fields
•	Name, type, assembly description, and available thickness.
•	Raw six-band absorption coefficients and their frequency bands.
•	Effective six-band coefficients used by the renderer.
•	Available porosity, airflow resistivity, tortuosity, viscous characteristic length, and thermal characteristic length.
•	Test standard, source, uncertainty, and measurement/modeling status.
Material model rules
•	Preserve raw values independently from effective-value clamping.
•	Validate effective absorption within 0.0..=1.0.
•	Record any modeling adjustment.
•	Missing acoustic parameters require an explicitly identified model or an unavailable-result state.
Loose reference profiles
•	Preserve compact instrument-family, microphone-style, signal-character, and room-category profiles.
•	Store approximate spectral distributions and supported decay/noise traits.
•	Use profile matches as bounded guidance with visible confidence.
•	Apply accepted guidance only in the stage that owns the relevant behavior.
Reference registry
•	Preserve available provenance from manufacturer specifications, Mic Archive, acoustic material measurements and standards, instrument-range references, RWC/SNDB references, audio mathematics references, and DDSP/spectral-modeling literature.
•	Preserve Leslie Gaston-Bird's Math Fundamentals for Audio as a mathematical reference where supplied.
•	Record access and licensing conditions for recovered third-party data.
•	Never represent private or unavailable reference data as recovered content.
11. Acoustic Mathematics
Internal units
•	Distances: meters.
•	Time: seconds and integer sample frames.
•	Angles: one documented internal convention, converted for degree displays.
•	Frequency: hertz.
•	Amplitude: linear sample amplitude unless a field explicitly identifies dB.
Existing propagation constant
speed_of_sound_m_per_s = 343.0
Direct and reflected delay
delay_seconds = path_length_m / speed_of_sound_m_per_s
delay_samples = delay_seconds * sample_rate_hz
•	Use a documented fractional-delay implementation when required.
Distance attenuation
•	Pressure/amplitude follows the modeled inverse-distance relationship.
•	Intensity follows the corresponding inverse-square relationship.
•	Do not apply inverse-square attenuation directly to waveform amplitude.
•	For positive distances:
level_change_db = -20.0 * log10(distance_2 / distance_1)
Passive reflection, per frequency band
reflection_pressure = sqrt(1.0 - effective_absorption)
path_reflection_pressure = product(reflection_pressure_for_each_bounce)
Geometry
•	Use actual path lengths, encountered surfaces, and arrival directions.
•	Apply source radiation only when an implemented model exists.
•	Keep a visual source-facing arrow separate from a radiation model.
Informational acoustic estimates
total_absorption_sabins = sum(surface_area_m2 * effective_absorption)
estimated_rt60_seconds = 0.161 * room_volume_m3 / total_absorption_sabins
axial_mode_hz = speed_of_sound_m_per_s * mode_index / (2.0 * dimension_m)
Estimate limits
•	Label RT60 and room modes as estimates.
•	Represent zero-absorption or invalid estimates explicitly without feeding NaN or infinity into DSP.
•	Mark closed-room estimates unavailable for open geometry.
•	Never replace geometric reflections with a generic RT60 tail.
Numeric verification
•	A 0.6096 m source-to-receiver distance produces approximately 1.777 ms delay.
•	Verify pressure factors independently from raw published coefficients.
•	Verify changes against actual geometry rather than screenshots.
12. Native Workspace and Display Boundaries
Workspace
•	Use a compact dark workstation with the audio canvas as the visual priority.
•	Provide native menus, transport, inspector, timeline, evidence controls, Plans, and 3D views.
•	Support resizing, docking, undocking, and reordering major panels.
•	Preserve usable panel minimums and remembered splitter sizes.
•	Preserve session state during layout changes.
Empty state
•	Show no track, measurements, generated files, or completed audit before an input is loaded.
•	Open a demo only through an explicit, clearly labeled action backed by actual files.
Import
•	Provide Open WAV, native file selection, file drop, and open-by-path fallback.
•	Show loading, ready, busy, and error states.
•	Show actual filename, duration, sample rate, and channel policy.
Main editing canvas
•	Display aligned original and current dry waveform/spectral views.
•	Keep a full-recording overview visible while editing a passage.
•	Share time ruler, playhead, and appropriate comparison scales.
•	Support seek, range selection, zoom-to-selection, and Fit Recording.
•	Label spectral regions above Nyquist as unavailable data.
Audio-derived graphics
•	Generate waveforms and spectrograms from actual corresponding audio buffers.
•	Preserve shared comparison scales.
•	Display uncertainty and cue overlays as evidence annotations.
Display-only controls
•	Inspector tabs, layer View, visual contrast, frequency-display limits, display precision, unit selection, overlays, projection, camera movement, zoom, fit commands, layout changes, and snap-toggle changes affect display state only.
•	Export precision and audio-processing settings remain separate controls.
Existing analysis layers
•	Preserve Pitch, Faint Ringing, Texture, and Breath Accent layers.
•	Retain Mute, Solo, gain, View, Reset, and editable-analysis save/load.
•	Treat Mute, Solo, and gain as explicit audio controls.
•	Treat View as display selection only.
•	Keep audition gains separate from measured analysis data.
•	Route accepted audio-affecting changes through the appropriate dry stage.
Inspector order and location
•	Dock the inspector on the left by default.
•	Order tabs as Source → Microphone → Materials → Room Size.
•	Keep source and microphone markers visible across tabs.
•	Preserve source selection, placement, aim, materials, room, and playback during tab or view changes.
13. Inspector Controls and Shared 3D Scene
Shared scene
•	Maintain one authoritative physical scene.
•	Use X for left/right, Y for depth, and Z for height.
•	Map room width to X, room length/depth to Y, and room height to Z.
•	Store physical dimensions in meters.
•	Derive every plan, 3D view, readout, and capture input from the same state.
Source tab
•	Provide Unknown, Instrument → family → source, and Human → Male / Female / Other-Ensemble selection.
•	Show published source data separately from effective synthesis parameters.
•	Keep a small recognizable source icon visible.
Source placement
•	Initialize the source at the footprint center and 1.524 m above the floor.
•	Permit independent X/Y/Z movement.
•	Provide optional center and 5 ft height locks, both off by default.
•	Preserve user placement during room resizing unless an explicit lock applies.
•	Reset Source restores center and 5 ft height without resetting microphones or materials.
•	Show exact position and floor-height readouts.
Microphone tab
•	Provide search and Brand → Model selection.
•	Preserve independent model, pattern, position, and aim per microphone slot.
•	Show the selected slot clearly.
•	Preserve position and aim when replacing a model.
•	Show effective pattern and response beside published specifications.
•	Keep a small pattern symbol and compact polar chart visible.
•	Use a separate toggle for larger pattern overlays.
Microphone placement
•	Top-view dragging changes X/Y and preserves Z.
•	Front-view dragging changes X/Z and preserves Y.
•	Provide exact numeric coordinates and live source-distance readouts.
•	Preserve aim during placement changes unless Follow Source is enabled.
•	Support grid snapping without changing existing positions when snap toggles.
•	Reset Mic restores a valid configured default or last valid position.
Microphone aim
•	Provide typed X tilt, Y roll, and Z heading.
•	Provide rotation control, right-angle snaps, and six grid directions.
•	Snap to Source performs one aim adjustment.
•	Follow Source updates aim when either source or microphone moves.
•	Aim commands never move the microphone.
•	Rotation release within 8 degrees of a grid axis snaps to that axis with brief visual feedback.
Reference capture setup
•	Retain a labeled SM7-style approximation approximately 2 ft from the source, aimed at the source, for repeatable capture checks.
•	Keep that setup inactive while microphone processing is bypassed.
Materials tab
•	Provide independent floor, ceiling, and four-wall assignments.
•	Provide search across the full recovered material catalog.
•	Show assembly information, raw coefficients, effective coefficients, available physical parameters, and provenance.
•	Changing one surface preserves every other surface assignment.
Room Size tab
•	Small: 10 × 10 ft footprint.
•	Medium: 20 × 20 ft footprint.
•	Large: 50 × 50 ft footprint.
•	Default height: 10 ft.
•	Open: ground only, initially shown within a 50 × 50 × 50 ft placement guide.
•	Provide editable width, length/depth, and height.
•	Link width and length initially; keep height independent initially.
•	Provide individual dimension links, Link All, and Unlink All.
•	Editing a linked dimension updates only the linked dimensions.
Display units
•	Support feet, inches, yards, millimeters, centimeters, and meters.
•	Convert presentation values without changing physical scene state.
Plans
•	Provide linked Top X/Y and Front X/Z views.
•	Share physical grid spacing, X divisions, pan, and zoom.
•	Preserve truthful depth and height outlines when dimensions differ.
•	Label rulers and physical distance per square.
3D Grid
•	Show the same floor, boundaries, source, microphone slots, and axes.
•	Bare-grid drag orbits.
•	Shift-drag on empty space pans.
•	Wheel zooms.
•	Orthographic, Perspective, and Fit View affect only the camera.
•	Empty-space interaction never edits source or microphone placement.
Placement guidance
•	Preserve source-to-microphone distance and direct-delay readouts.
•	Keep multi-microphone distance-ratio guidance informational.
•	Do not present a distance ratio as guaranteed freedom from comb filtering.
•	Preserve supported ORTF, X/Y, and spaced-pair helpers.
•	Use ORTF's 17 cm spacing and 110-degree included angle where that preset is implemented.
•	Define spaced-pair half-spacing and total spacing separately.
•	Show actual resulting geometry before treating a helper as applied.
•	Displaying guidance changes no audio; applying placement changes the scene.
14. Playback, Preview, Export, and Review
Transport
•	Provide Play/Pause, numeric position, and Space shortcut when focus permits.
•	Let the playback engine own the playhead.
•	Never seek because a rounded time display redraws.
•	Preserve logical time when switching Original and Regenerated audition.
Comparison monitoring
•	Keep original monitoring outside the processing graph.
•	Level-match audition copies without modifying source or exported audio.
•	Retain Difference monitoring only when the buffer contains the actual aligned generated-minus-reference comparison.
•	Never use comparison buffers as reconstruction or capture inputs.
Background update
•	Debounce substantive live adjustments.
•	Render outside the audio callback.
•	Crossfade validated replacement buffers at the current logical playhead.
•	Preserve playback position during microphone, room, or source rerendering.
Preview versus export
•	Preview controls MUST NOT silently change export settings.
•	Export commands MUST identify the exact stage and current revision.
•	Exporting Stage 2 MUST include accepted surgical fixes.
•	Exporting any stage MUST use a complete, valid result for that stage.
Export selection
•	Preserve individual, selected-subset, and all-requested-output selection.
•	Present available stage outputs and existing generated-layer outputs explicitly.
•	Do not require a bundled suite of named character variants.
Export files
•	Write usable WAV files to new destinations.
•	Preserve logical timeline alignment.
•	Preserve declared acoustic tails rather than silently trimming tails.
•	Record sample format, stage settings, latency, physical delay, and provenance.
•	Keep ordinary dry and capture outputs unmastered with editing headroom.
•	Permit finishing behavior only through an explicitly enabled plugin.
•	Never apply hidden normalization during bypass or surgical acceptance.
Original export
•	An explicitly requested original copy remains an unchanged reference asset.
•	Keep the reference copy separate from generated pipeline outputs.
Review state
•	Mark generated audio Candidate until a human listening review is recorded.
•	Bind review status to the exact effective dry revision.
•	Invalidate the corresponding review when committed dry audio changes.
•	Permit candidate audition and stage export without claiming approval.
•	Numerical measurements cannot create Reviewed or Accepted status.
•	Report unavailable listening assessment honestly.
15. Error Handling and Real-time Safety
Input validation
•	Reject non-finite values and invalid dimensions with actionable errors.
•	Handle unsupported formats, corrupt files, missing catalog data, and incompatible processor settings without panics.
•	Define and expose actual duration, format, memory, and processing limits.
•	Never silently apply historical excerpt limits to a full-track request.
Geometry validation
•	Reject invalid closed-room placements while retaining the last valid state.
•	Treat an open-space guide as a view aid rather than a closed boundary.
•	Handle coincident source/receiver positions explicitly without singular distance gain.
•	Validate material coefficients before reflection calculations.
Audio thread
•	Perform no blocking file access, contended locking, or avoidable allocation inside the playback callback.
•	Handle device removal, sample-rate changes, and underruns explicitly.
•	Prevent NaN/Inf propagation and stale-render races.
Rust implementation
•	Propagate meaningful Result values.
•	Avoid runtime panic paths for recoverable user or device errors.
•	Keep unsafe code minimal and document the upheld safety invariant.
•	Use appropriate numerical tolerances for mathematical comparisons.
•	Use exact sample or hash comparisons for deterministic and bypass contracts.
Resource handling
•	Respect file permissions and validate user-supplied paths.
•	Protect original assets and committed outputs from accidental overwrite.
•	Clean temporary results after cancellation or failure.
•	Bound processing memory for long takes.
Dependency integration
•	Adopt shared Rust infrastructure only for an identified application need.
•	Keep shape extraction, regeneration, catalogs, and capture mathematics controlled by SpectraCraft.
•	Pin compatible craft-libs dependencies to one revision when adopted.
•	Preserve required attribution and verify actual crate availability/licenses.
•	Require no runtime access to a dependency repository.
16. Required Verification
Baseline
•	Inspect manifests, lockfile, source, tests, reproduction instructions, migration records, and supplied acceptance documents before engine changes.
•	Record actual repository capabilities and baseline test results.
•	Separate structural refactoring from intentional behavior changes.
Source isolation
•	Render from a saved cue packet in a run without original-file access.
•	Render a second output from the same packet and effective configuration.
•	Verify sample-identical deterministic dry output.
•	Verify original checksums before and after processing.
Performance controls
•	Use Rust-generated known controls for:
o	weak fundamentals
o	genuine octave jumps
o	continuous slides
o	repeated notes
o	overlapping ringing
o	quiet attacks
o	silence
o	unrelated clicks and chirps.
•	Test multiple sample rates and held-out controls.
Evidence reporting
•	Report jointly resolved frames, agreed frames, disagreement, octave errors, and unresolved frames separately.
•	Show agreement denominators for jointly resolved and all evaluated frames.
•	Never octave-fold genuine errors away.
•	Keep envelope, pitch, headroom, and listening findings separate.
Full-track verification
•	Verify the complete supplied guitar reference using actual file identity.
•	Make the 20–27 s passage and approximately 25.5 s transition reachable.
•	Preserve unresolved transition evidence.
•	Never substitute another decode or duration without separate provenance.
Stage verification
•	Check declared input/output ownership and every bypass contract.
•	Check all optional stages bypassed yields the current dry buffer unchanged.
•	Check room-only capture uses neutral pickup and valid geometry.
•	Check direct and reflected paths receive each acoustic operation once.
•	Check downstream changes preserve dry caches.
•	Check enabled-stage errors cannot become silent bypasses.
Surgical verification
•	Check rectangle/range creation, movement, every resize edge, and deletion.
•	Check selection-only operations preserve committed audio.
•	Check stale candidate rejection and independent undo/redo.
•	Check samples outside accepted temporal bounds remain bit-identical.
•	Check frequency-limited edits cannot silently become full-band edits.
•	Check accepted fixes appear in dry export.
•	Check downstream invalidation follows acceptance rather than selection.
Scene and catalog verification
•	Check full catalog counts against package manifests.
•	Select entries near the end of each catalog and inspect actual loaded data.
•	Check microphone model changes preserve pose.
•	Check one material assignment preserves the other five assignments.
•	Check actual capture changes when an effective enabled parameter changes.
•	Check equivalent implemented profiles are not falsely reported as distinct.
•	Check Plans, 3D, numeric fields, and capture geometry remain synchronized.
Display verification
•	Check orbit, pan, zoom, projection, units, overlays, tabs, panel changes, layer View, and snap toggles preserve audio samples and render fingerprints.
•	Check source/microphone positions survive camera and layout operations.
Application verification
•	Exercise empty state, import, complete timeline navigation, regeneration, region preview, Apply/Undo, stage bypass, playback/seek, project save/reopen, and aligned WAV export.
•	Verify generated plots and reported measurements against actual artifacts.
